{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261001T014638Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "4ed2607a5b2ebbf927d96b8d", "title": "Forecasting space weather risks on power grids", "url": "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/forecasting-space-weather-risks-on-power-grids/", "source": "Microsoft Research", "published": "2026-09-30T16:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.93, "worthiness": 0.87, "worthiness_confidence": 0.57, "final_score": 0.87, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "feed_full_text", "text_chars": 8783, "delivered": true, "summary": "Microsoft Research developed a machine learning pipeline that forecasts geomagnetically induced current (GIC) risks across 66,935 U.S. substations by combining solar-wind forecasts, AE and Dst indices, and local geological data. The system detects nearly 80% of major space-weather events and provides 30-60 minute advance warning, enabling grid operators to take protective actions before impacts occur. For AI engineers building critical infrastructure systems, this demonstrates how physics-informed machine learning can translate raw forecasts into actionable, location-specific risk estimates. The key insight: gradient-boosting models outperform traditional approaches when grounded in domain physics and local environmental factors.", "jev_ms": 500.5, "jev_input_tokens": 2365, "jev_cost": 9.933e-05, "llm_ms": 3602.5, "llm_cost": 0.002761, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261001T014638Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "cfe32cdb08fcc91ab019a475", "title": "Why Dwarkesh is Wrong about Computer Use + How OpenAI shipped its Jev competitor in 1 Week", "url": "https://www.latent.space/p/devday-2026", "source": "Latent.Space", "published": "2026-09-30T22:23:40+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.775, "worthiness_confidence": 0.9, "final_score": 0.775, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "feed_full_text", "text_chars": 57493, "delivered": true, "summary": "OpenAI shipped major Computer Use and API improvements at DevDay: Dots with personal cloud computers, GPT-6.1 Sol (5x cheaper than Astra for Computer Use), Agents API with integrated Computer Use, and Decisions API for fast classification. Computer Use now debugs failures, combines screenshots with accessibility data and generated code, and completes some tasks faster than humans. The key takeaway: Computer Use has fundamentally changed—agents now write JavaScript to perform multiple actions simultaneously rather than single steps, making it 180 degrees different from months ago and enabling superhuman software automation.", "jev_ms": 578.6, "jev_input_tokens": 17678, "jev_cost": 0.000742476, "llm_ms": 3863.6, "llm_cost": 0.017636, "errors": []}
